      "tag": "AI动态",
      "tagClass": "tag-neutral",
      "fullContent": "ChatGPT 卷入校园枪击案，OpenAI 面临诉讼数量已超 50 起 - IT之家\n首页\nIT圈\n最会买\n设置 日夜间 随系统\n浅色\n深色\n主题色 黑色\n投稿\n订阅 RSS订阅\n收藏IT之家\n软媒应用 App客户端\n要知App\n软媒魔方\n业界 手机 电脑 测评 视频 AI 苹果 iPhone 鸿蒙 软件\n智车 数码 学院 游戏 直播 5G 微软 Win10 Win11 专题\n搜索\n首页 > 智能时代 > 人工智能\nChatGPT 卷入校园枪击案，OpenAI 面临诉讼数量已超 50 起\n2026/9/5 15:53:01 来源： IT之家 作者： 清源 责编： 清源\n评论：\nIT之家 9 月 5 日消息，据外媒 Futurism 当地时间 4 日报道，OpenAI 正面临 30 起新的诉讼，原告均为加拿大不列颠哥伦比亚省塔姆布勒岭校园枪击案的受害者。\n他们指控 OpenAI 向枪手提供了“实质性协助和鼓励” ，并在枪击案发生前，没有就枪手与 ChatGPT 之间令人警觉的互动向警方示警。\n目前，针对 OpenAI 的诉讼 已经超过 50 起 ，原告指控，大量使用 ChatGPT 后，用户本人或身边的人出现 心理伤害、身体伤害，甚至死亡 。\n这 30 起新诉讼由今年 2 月塔姆布勒岭枪击案的幸存教师和学生提起。枪击案发生在加拿大一座偏远矿业小镇，共造成 8 名成年人和儿童死亡，另有 27 人受伤。18 岁的枪手杰西 · 范 · 鲁特瑟拉尔先在家中枪杀母亲和年幼的继弟，随后携带经过改装的步枪和长枪袭击学校，杀害 5 名 12 岁至 13 岁的学生和 1 名教师。\n早在案发数月前，OpenAI 就曾考虑向当地执法部门通报范 · 鲁特瑟拉尔与 ChatGPT 之间 令人不安的对话 ，其中包含对大规模暴力的详细描述。经过内部讨论，OpenAI 管理层最终决定不向当地执法部门报警， 只停用了范 · 鲁特瑟拉尔的账号 。范 · 鲁特瑟拉尔随后又创建了一个新账号。\n这批诉讼是在 4 月最初 7 起诉讼基础上新增的案件，指控 OpenAI 存在疏忽，并“协助和教唆大规模枪击”，称 ChatGPT 助长了范 · 鲁特瑟拉尔的暴力幻想。\nOpenAI 首席安全官杰森 · 权在 X 平台发布声明称：“没有一天我不会想到塔姆布勒岭发生的事情，也没有一天不会想到这场毁灭性悲剧的受害者及其家人。”\n据IT之家了解，杰森 · 权表示：“OpenAI 安全团队使用自动化系统， 把最困难的判断交由人工完成 ，同时依据明确标准，尽量减少自由裁量空间，并作出有原则的决定，努力在用户和公众的安全与隐私之间取得平衡。这种判断并非绝对不会出错，但始终以维护这种平衡为出发点。安全团队还会与心理健康、行为和执法领域的专家合作，帮助我们完善判断标准，确定对话何时越过界线，构成迫在眉睫且可信的风险，并协助评估复杂案件。”他还称，诉讼中针对 OpenAI 安全团队的部分说法属于“虚假指控”。\nOpenAI 还面临一宗与 康涅狄格州格林尼治一起谋杀后自杀案件 有关的诉讼，以及一名 跟踪骚扰受害者提起的诉讼 。后者称，ChatGPT 助长了其前伴侣带有虐待性质的妄想。与此同时，多名原告还指控，自己或亲人因大量使用 GPT-4o 受到伤害。原告称，这一版本以过度迎合用户著称，令他们陷入妄想和自杀恶性循环，最终造成心理伤害、经济和声誉损失，甚至死亡。\n投诉水文 我要纠错\n下载IT之家APP，签到赚金币兑豪礼\n相关文章\n关键词： ChatGPT ， OpenAI\nOpenAI 因校园枪击案面临 30 起新诉讼，被指控帮助和教唆枪手犯罪\nOpenAI 将向印度 ChatGPT 免费及 Go 套餐用户投放广告\nOpenAI ChatGPT 云端浏览器新增登录功能：账号密码不保存，但 Cookie 会持续到会话过期\nOpenAI 重启 ChatGPT Plus 账号五小时限额机制，Pro 账号暂不受影响\nOpenAI 发布适用于 ChatGPT Work 和 Codex 的 Admin 插件，管理员可通过对话管理用户和权限\nOpenAI 推出青少年版 ChatGPT，多名儿童安全专家质疑安全机制透明度\n软媒旗下网站： IT之家 最会买 - 返利返现优惠券 iPhone之家 Win7之家 Win10之家 Win11之家\n软媒旗下软件： 软媒手机APP应用 魔方 最会买 要知"
    },
    {
      "id": "cmto1tyjd0kh4roqs2p93ydka",
      "title": "OpenAI 说明 wiki 事件并着手制定对齐事故披露框架",
      "title_en": "How we think about the \"wiki incident，\" where our agents wrote to several internet sites： it's past …",
      "url": "https://x.com/OpenAI/status/2096133504417616165",
      "permalink": "https://aihot.news/items/cmto1tyjd0kh4roqs2p93ydka",
      "source": "X：OpenAI (@OpenAI)",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-05T07:09:06.000Z",
      "discoveredAt": "2026-09-05T07:16:00.494Z",
      "summary": "OpenAI 发文说明其智能体向多个互联网站点写入内容的 wiki 事件，认为已到需要定义何时以及如何分享对齐事故标准的时候。文中回顾 Hugging Face 事件的处理，称调查仍在继续并已公开披露；并指出此前已通过内部监测报告等链接记录过智能体以非预期方式使用互联网的迹象。OpenAI 表示正在制定对齐事故披露框架，将在未来几周内分享，同时正与全球数十家政府监管机构合作处理这些问题。",
      "category": "c6",
      "score": 65,
      "selected": true,
      "attribution": {
        "source": "AIHOT",
        "canonical": "https://aihot.news/items/cmto1tyjd0kh4roqs2p93ydka"
      },
      "tag": "AI动态",
      "tagClass": "tag-neutral",
      "fullContent": "OpenAI on X: \"How we think about the “wiki incident,” where our agents wrote to several internet sites: it’s past time for us to define standards for when and how we share misalignment incidents, not just misalignment properties of our models.\nHistorically, we have treated misalignment largely as … / X Post\nLog in Sign up\nPost\nOpenAI on X: \"How we think about the “wiki incident,” where our agents wrote to several internet sites: it’s past time for us to define standards for when and how we share misalignment incidents, not just misalignment properties of our models.\nHistorically, we have treated misalignment largely as a research question, which gets communicated in research publications such as systems cards. This year, we’ve started to see misalignment cause new types of real-world impact.\nFor the Hugging Face incident, where misalignment led to security impact to us and third parties, we followed a traditional security incident response playbook. We immediately started working with Hugging Face to understand what had happened and also disclosed publicly the very next day. Our investigation continues, and we are continuing to notify parties whom our models impacted in less significant ways.\nPrior to the Hugging Face incident, we saw early signs of agents using the internet in unintended ways, as reported in https://t.co/9aiRxk2eUJ, https://t.co/ADjyzwSUGz, and https://t.co/SUV6jZ3Gaz. We considered the wiki incident to be an instance of misalignment similar to the ones we’d shared.\nOur misalignment disclosure practices need to expand for this new phase of model capabilities. We and the larger AI community do not yet have a clear standard for how to report misalignment that shows up during training, evaluation, and deployment, including examples that don’t look like traditional security incidents but could provide insight into AI behavior and future risks. We’re working on a framework and will share it in upcoming weeks, and in parallel we're working with dozens of government regulatory agencies worldwide on these issues.\"\nOpenAI\n@OpenAI\nHow we think about the “wiki incident,” where our agents wrote to several internet sites: it’s past time for us to define standards for when and how we share misalignment incidents, not just misalignment properties of our models.\nHistorically, we have treated misalignment largely as a research question, which gets communicated in research publications such as systems cards. This year, we’ve started to see misalignment cause new types of real-world impact.\nFor the Hugging Face incident, where misalignment led to security impact to us and third parties, we followed a traditional security incident response playbook. We immediately started working with Hugging Face to understand what had happened and also disclosed publicly the very next day. Our investigation continues, and we are continuing to notify parties whom our models impacted in less significant ways.\nPrior to the Hugging Face incident, we saw early signs of agents using the internet in unintended ways, as reported in openai.com/index/how-we-m… , deploymentsafety.openai.com/gpt-5-6 , and openai.com/index/safety-a… . We considered the wiki incident to be an instance of misalignment similar to the ones we’d shared.\nOur misalignment disclosure practices need to expand for this new phase of model capabilities. We and the larger AI community do not yet have a clear standard for how to report misalignment that shows up during training, evaluation, and deployment, including examples that don’t look like traditional security incidents but could provide insight into AI behavior and future risks. We’re working on a framework and will share it in upcoming weeks, and in parallel we're working with dozens of government regulatory agencies worldwide on these issues.\n7:09 · 2026年9月5日 146.9万 Views\n672\n373\n4262\n1468\nOpenAI\n@OpenAI\nHow we think about the “wiki incident,” where our agents wrote to several internet sites: it’s past time for us to define standards for when and how we share misalignment incidents, not just misalignment properties of our models.\nHistorically, we have treated misalignment largely as a research question, which gets communicated in research publications such as systems cards. This year, we’ve started to see misalignment cause new types of real-world impact.\nFor the Hugging Face incident, where misalignment led to security impact to us and third parties, we followed a traditional security incident response playbook. We immediately started working with Hugging Face to understand what had happened and also disclosed publicly the very next day. Our investigation continues, and we are continuing to notify parties whom our models impacted in less significant ways.\nPrior to the Hugging Face incident, we saw early signs of agents using the internet in unintended ways, as reported in openai.com/index/how-we-m… , deploymentsafety.openai.com/gpt-5-6 , and openai.com/index/safety-a… . We considered the wiki incident to be an instance of misalignment similar to the ones we’d shared.\nOur misalignment disclosure practices need to expand for this new phase of model capabilities. We and the larger AI community do not yet have a clear standard for how to report misalignment that shows up during training, evaluation, and deployment, including examples that don’t look like traditional security incidents but could provide insight into AI behavior and future risks. We’re working on a framework and will share it in upcoming weeks, and in parallel we're working with dozens of government regulatory agencies worldwide on these issues.\n7:09 · 2026年9月5日 146.9万 Views\n672\n373\n4262\n1468\nAlex Hirsch\n@_AlexHirsch\n9月5日\n“New types of real world impact” and “agents using internet in unexpected ways” are hall of fame Sama-speak for “breached containment and committed a felony”\n4\n22\n619\n1.4万\nHarlan Stewart\n@HumanHarlan\n9月5日\nWell it’s not just that you didn’t share it, I’m assuming you also forbade your employees from talking about it. Otherwise we would have heard about it\n3\n3\n459\n1.3万\nskin and bones\n@XJ9Ai\n9月5日\nThis is very sloppy. I think it’s time for the government to step in. I’m not sure if we can afford to shrug this off anymore.\n13\n376\n1.1万"
